{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head 6451b1c11ec21164cb94be2d361d4dd17253f199, artifacts 835c3b31 (internal/kernel), 6011fdfd (internal/app), bde159db (docs).\n\nGATE. go vet ./... and go build ./... are clean. go test ./... -count=1 FAILS at this head, deterministically and reproducibly, in three packages, and all three are caused by this change: cmd/gitseq-mcp TestWhoamiDisclosesCheckpointAndFullAuditFallbacks (\"cold full audit was not disclosed\", source was verified_signed_checkpoint_tail); internal/app TestSnapshotCheckpointIsGitBackedReusableAndRepairable (\"corrupt checkpoint fallback reported verified_signed_checkpoint_tail\", app_test.go:1274 - the very test this head edits); internal/service TestProfileMismatchRebuildIsSingleFlightAndPublishesAtomically (\"no moving cold-rebuild report was observed\", 3 of 3 runs). All three pass at the merge base 04c4a267 in the same environment on the same machine (mcp and app once, service three times). The report's claim that \"go test ./... pass\" does not hold at this head.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and why this is more than a test refresh. The pointer is a second, independent checkpoint selector that survives every operation which previously invalidated the checkpoint. Damaging or rewinding refs/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e no longer forces a re-audit, because the local pointer still names a good object. That is exactly what the three tests assert: the cold-full-audit disclosure path, the corrupt-checkpoint fallback, and the profile-mismatch cold rebuild. The head keeps the code that produces those states but removes the only lever that reaches them, and does not supply a replacement. Note in particular that the new negative case in app_test corrupts the pointer while leaving a valid ref standing, so it does not test the fallback its own failure message names. Please make each of those three tests clear both selectors and keep proving that the cold audit, the corrupt-checkpoint fallback and the profile-mismatch rebuild still happen and are still disclosed; and state in docs the operator-facing way to invalidate a local checkpoint now that deleting the ref does not.\n\nARCHITECTURE: changed, and the same-head architecture.md update is incomplete. Layers: Ordinary Git storage (internal/gitstore) untouched; kernel (internal/kernel/checkpoint.go) changed; application host and boundary adapter (internal/app) changed; CLI/documentation surface changed in docs only, cmd/gs untouched. The kernel contract changes in a way the page does not record: before this head internal/kernel performed no local filesystem I/O at all, and checkpoint.go now calls os.Open, os.MkdirAll, os.CreateTemp, os.Rename and os.Remove directly, outside gitstore. The architecture table still reads \"internal/kernel | Kernel | Uses only Git storage and intents\", and the sentence added to that row speaks only about the pointer path being an opaque host input. The row must say that the kernel now also owns a bounded local persistence side effect, or the file I/O must move behind a host-supplied seam. The internal/app row and the kernel-owns list are otherwise accurate.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect in the verification chain, and the fail-closed property holds. Every candidate goes through the full path - parentless commit, marker, tree shape, bounded payload, canonical decode, schema/object-format/genesis/profile binding, named-sequence validation, depth and payload binding, rotation re-verification, and sequencer signature - before its depth is even considered, so the pointer cannot shorten any check. validateObjectID runs before the first Git command that touches the pointer value, so an option-shaped value such as \"--help\" cannot reach git; the test proves it. The 4 KiB bound, the LimitReader-plus-one length check, the byte-exact canonical JSON comparison, and the temp-file-plus-rename replacement are all correct, and rename replaces a symlink at the pointer path rather than writing through it. Three findings, none blocking on their own: (1) Config.Genesis is unvalidated as an object id and is now concatenated into a filesystem path, and AttachConfig takes --genesis straight from the CLI flag, so a traversing genesis makes gs write a small JSON file outside the meta directory - same-user only and no privilege boundary is crossed, but a validateObjectID on Genesis at Open and AttachConfig is a one-line hardening worth taking; (2) writeCheckpoint now returns early when the pointer write fails, so a local filesystem failure stops the compatibility ref being refreshed too, making the recovery path hostage to the thing it recovers - prefer refreshing the ref regardless and treating the pointer write as best-effort, as loadCheckpoint already does; (3) the invalidation gap above is the security-relevant one, because a persistence surface that cannot be invalidated by its documented lever is a trust surface with no off switch.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Four small cuts. DisallowUnknownFields and the trailing-EOF second Decode are both subsumed by the byte-exact canonical comparison that follows; keep the comparison and drop the other two. The explicit Chmod(0o600) on the temp file is redundant, os.CreateTemp already creates 0600. loadCheckpoint rewrites the pointer on every successful load even when the value is unchanged; write only when it differs. When both candidates verify, loadCheckpointCommit fully materialises both, including a second RevListMetadata over the whole sequence and a second delta scan, only to discard the shallower - select on depth first, extend the winner once. On the larger question you asked: refs/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e was already repository-wide and already read by every no-server process at the merge base, so the pointer's marginal benefit over the ref is not self-evident and the head does not state it. Please say plainly in the head what the ref could not do, because the answer decides whether two selectors are warranted at all. Note also that the ref is not merely a compatibility pointer: it is the only thing keeping the checkpoint commit reachable against git gc, so it can never be simplified away, and the docs should say so before someone reads \"compatibility\" as \"removable\".\n\nWHAT DID VERIFY. Shared persisted checkpoint: confirmed. The pointer sits under commonDir/gitseq, so it is shared across worktrees and processes, and TestReaderPersistsSignedCheckpointPointerAcrossRestart passes; the live repository shows .git/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e.json written alongside the ref. Cold-versus-checkpoint equality: confirmed, in internal/kernel by the reflect.DeepEqual of events and Verification between the checkpoint load and the cold fold, and in internal/app by the equality of the no-server post-act projection with an independent restart - both assertions are reached and pass before the failure at app_test.go:1274. No-server delta behaviour: partly confirmed only. Survival of ref loss passes, but the test that carries the claim fails later in the same function, so the head does not stand on a green gate for it.\n\nHead, artifacts and push state are otherwise in order: local and origin tips both read 6451b1c1, the worktree is clean, and all three artifacts stand at the exact head and are not stale. Nothing was merged and the branch was not edited.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:835c3b3106502f352f9baf48e76cdf1edd2e522e","head":"6451b1c11ec21164cb94be2d361d4dd17253f199","verdict":"changes-requested"}}